# Reduce rules mapping an all-atom protein onto Martini 3.0.0 coarse-grain
# beads. Generated by data/martini3/prepare_martini3_data.py -- do not edit
# by hand. Pair it with forcefield/martini3.nbi.ff, which declares every
# grain name this file produces, terminal qualifiers included.
#
# GRAIN NAMES ARE MARTINI'S BEAD TYPES: "SP2 ALA N CA C O" says alanine's
# backbone bead is of type SP2. A grain name is the force-field key
# (ReduceRule::forcefield_key), so the companion .nbi.ff is Martini's own
# type table -- 90 keys for these 207 rules -- and "name TC5" in a viewer
# selects every aromatic ring vertex. Two spellings are forced by the engine
# and both were measured: a type repeating inside one residue takes a _2 or
# _3 suffix, and at a free chain end the name stays the INTERIOR type while
# the qualified key carries Martini's terminal parameters. See _grain_names
# in the generator for what each costs when ignored -- 10 beads with no
# spring at all, and 6 springs lost silently at the chain ends.
#
# Unlike data/martini/reducerules/martini.grp, this is a REAL reduction: it
# takes an atomic structure and builds the beads itself, so martinize is not
# in the pipeline. Atom names are AMBER's.
#
# HEAVY ATOMS ONLY, AND THAT IS THE ACCURATE CHOICE, not a shortcut. A
# Martini bead is the MASS-weighted average of its atoms (martinize2 runs
# vermouth's DoAverageBead with center_weight = "mass"); a BioSpring grain is
# measure::centroid, an unweighted mean. Over the 401 beads of
# example/001_GKinase/model.pdb, listing the heavy atoms only lands 0.028 A
# from the true Martini bead in the median and 0.415 A at worst, where
# listing Martini's weight-1 atoms with their hydrogens lands 0.342 A and
# 0.730 A -- twelve times further, because an unweighted centroid gives a
# hydrogen the same pull as a carbon and a mass-weighted one gives it 1/13 of
# that. The worst residual is on the sulfur beads: CYS SC1 is CB at 12 Da and
# SG at 32.
#
# The input therefore does NOT need hydrogens. Any it has are claimed by no
# rule and reported one per atom, which is correct and expected -- they are
# not part of any bead here.
#
# A free chain end gets its own ":NTER"/":CTER" rules, which REPLACE the
# unqualified ones rather than adding to them, and its own force-field keys
# so that it carries Martini's terminal charge. Their atom lists differ from
# the interior one only by OXT at the C end, the N end having no heavy atom
# of its own.
#
#
# --- Interior residues ---
SP2 ALA N CA C O
TC3 ALA CB
P2 CYS N CA C O
TC6 CYS CB SG
P2 ASP N CA C O
SQ5n ASP CB CG OD1 OD2
P2 ASH N CA C O
SP2 ASH CB CG OD1 OD2
P2 GLU N CA C O
Q5n GLU CB CG CD OE1 OE2
P2 GLH N CA C O
P2_2 GLH CB CG CD OE1 OE2
P2 PHE N CA C O
SC4 PHE CB CG CD1
TC5 PHE CE1
TC5_2 PHE CZ CD2 CE2
SP1 GLY N CA C O
P2 HIS N CA C O
TC4 HIS CB CG
TN5a HIS CD2 NE2
TN6d HIS ND1 CE1
P2 HIE N CA C O
TC4 HIE CB CG
TN6d HIE CD2 NE2
TN5a HIE ND1 CE1
P2 HIP N CA C O
TC4 HIP CB CG
TP1dq HIP CD2 NE2
TP1dq_2 HIP ND1 CE1
P2 ILE N CA C O
SC2 ILE CB CG1 CG2 CD1
P2 LYS N CA C O
SC3 LYS CB CG CD
SQ4p LYS CE NZ
P2 LYN N CA C O
SC3 LYN CB CG CD
SN6d LYN CE NZ
P2 LEU N CA C O
SC2 LEU CB CG CD1 CD2
P2 MET N CA C O
C6 MET CB CG SD CE
P2 ASN N CA C O
SP5 ASN CB CG OD1 ND2
P1 HYP N CA C O
P1_2 HYP CB CG CD OD1
SP2a PRO N CA C O
SC3 PRO CB CG CD
P2 GLN N CA C O
P5 GLN CB CG CD OE1 NE2
P2 ARG N CA C O
SC3 ARG CB CG CD
SQ3p ARG NE CZ NH1 NH2
P2 SER N CA C O
TP1 SER CB OG
P2 THR N CA C O
SP1 THR CB OG1 CG2
SP2 VAL N CA C O
SC3 VAL CB CG1 CG2
P2 TRP N CA C O
TC4 TRP CB CG
TN6d TRP CD1 NE1
TC5 TRP CE2 CD2
TC5_2 TRP CE3 CZ3
TC5_3 TRP CZ2 CH2
P2 TYR N CA C O
TC4 TYR CB CG
TC5 TYR CD1 CE1
TC5_2 TYR CD2 CE2
TN6 TYR CZ OH
#
# --- Free N-terminus ---
SP2 ALA:NTER N CA C O
TC3 ALA:NTER CB
P2 CYS:NTER N CA C O
TC6 CYS:NTER CB SG
P2 ASP:NTER N CA C O
